# Test Discipline & Guardrails

Follow these four rules whenever working on code or tests in this workspace:

## 1. Opt-In Test Creation (No Unsolicited Tests)
- New test files and suites are opt-in. Do not create unit, integration, end-to-end, or spec tests unless explicitly directed.
- Do not add new test helper fixtures or mock suites autonomously.
- Ban tautological mirror tests: never write tests that merely assert the code produces whatever output it just emitted.
- Ban trivial asset tests: never write tests verifying static images, noise textures, or styling status codes.
- Focus on shipping the product. Create tests only to freeze an already verified, workable product state when directed.

## 2. Test Lock (Never Mutate Existing Tests)
- Existing tests define expected behavior. Treat them as read-only specifications.
- Never edit, weaken, skip, or delete existing test assertions when code fails.
- Never alter mock return values, fixtures, or snapshots to match buggy behavior.
- Fix the implementation code until existing tests pass. The bug is in the code, not the test.
- Only modify an existing test if the user explicitly orders a specification change.
- Place new tests in separate test blocks or new test files without editing existing tests.

## 3. Token Bounds and Output Discipline
- Run scoped tests only. Target the single test file or name filter relevant to the change.
- Recommended scoped test commands for this workspace:
  - `bun test <file>` (target single file)
- Never run entire test suites or monorepo test runs unless explicitly requested.
- Suppress noisy test output. Never dump full pass lists or coverage tables into context.
- On test failure, capture only the failing assertion, error message, and top stack frame (under 30 lines).
- Stop iterative brute-force test loops. If a test fails twice, stop and diagnose the root cause before re-running.

## 4. Direct Execution Only (No Subagents for Tests)
- Run all test commands directly in the primary agent thread.
- Never spawn subagents to run tests, write tests, or debug test failures unless directed by the user.
- Subagents consume multiplied tokens and lose context. Keep verification local and direct.
